From 62d196b3ed4ced33c75d11becf57e9638e56bfc8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ekkapot Charoenwanit <ekkapot.c@tggs.kmutnb.ac.th>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 21:30:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] examples/lorenz: a chaotic-flow propagator that actually
 chunks

Every other example either does not chunk or chunks a handful of times.  This
one drives the machinery the library exists for: a 2000-step RK4 integration
of Lorenz-63 makes a tape that is deep and narrow, a 180 kB budget breaks it
into 148 partitions, and those are dealt round the ranks -- 74/74 at np=2,
37 each at np=4, 49/50 at np=3, 21/22 at np=7.

IT ASSERTS THAT THIS HAPPENED.  Fewer than eight partitions is a failure, and
so is every partition landing on one rank when np>1.  Maxwell's SVEGP-22
point was that an example can stop exercising the replay path with no way to
notice; in a pipeline there is a second way to stop testing -- chunk, but
chunk onto a single rank -- and both are now failures rather than
observations.  Verified by running it with a 100 MB budget, where both fire.

THE SCIENCE.  The Jacobian of the final state with respect to the initial
state is the tangent-linear propagator M(T), the object at the centre of
4D-Var data assimilation and of Lyapunov analysis, and a genuinely hard thing
to get by finite differences: the system is chaotic, so a perturbation grows
like e^(0.9t) and the step that is small enough to stay linear is too small to
stay accurate.  That is the practical argument for AD, and it is why this
example checks itself against two things that are not finite differences.

  1  THE VARIATIONAL EQUATION, dM/dt = J(u)M, integrated alongside the state
     in plain doubles with no AD anywhere.  For a Runge-Kutta method the
     tangent-linear of the scheme IS the scheme applied to the variational
     equation with the same stage values, so this agrees to MACHINE
     PRECISION rather than to discretisation order.  Observed 4.5e-15 to
     8.4e-15 at every rank count; the tolerance is 1e-11.

  2  LIOUVILLE'S THEOREM.  Lorenz's field has constant divergence
     -(s+1+b) = -13.667, so det M(T) = exp(-13.667 T) exactly -- an analytic
     identity constraining all nine entries jointly, which no agreement with
     another numerical propagator would satisfy by accident.

AND THE SECOND CHECK FOUND ITS OWN LIMIT, which is the part worth reading.
det M is the product of the three singular values: one grows like e^(0.9T)
while the product shrinks like e^(-13.67T), so the determinant is a tiny
number formed by cancellation between entries that are not tiny.  Computing
it costs eps*||M||^3/|det M| relative -- 5e-14 at T=0.25, 1e-11 at T=0.5,
4e-7 at T=1, and LARGER THAN ONE at T=2.  Measured at T=2: the residual was
8e-2 at one rank and 2.0 at four, while the tangent-linear check held at
3e-15 at every rank count.  The determinant disagreeing across rank counts
there is not the library getting a different answer; it is the same one-ulp
reassociation the invariants suite pins, amplified by 1e14.

So the example works that bound out from the Jacobian it actually computed,
adds the RK4 discretisation error, and asserts the identity only where double
precision can resolve it -- reporting the check as unresolvable, with the
numbers, past that point.  A test that quietly widens its tolerance until it
passes is worse than no test.

The finite-time Lyapunov exponent is reported too, labelled as transient: one
tape at T=0.5 gives 5.5, and the asymptotic 0.906 needs renormalisation over
t ~ 100.  Reporting it as an indicator rather than asserting it is the honest
treatment.

The model is one template over the scalar type, so the active and passive
integrations are demonstrably the same arithmetic rather than two
transcriptions of it.  The section uses run_tape() and so contains no
try/catch, and allocates nothing, so the throw has nothing to strand.

Added to the fast group, to make ranks, and to make sanitize.  0.3 s at np=2.
Whole suite green at 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 7 ranks; clean under AddressSanitizer
and LeakSanitizer; zero library warnings.
